<section class="phero"><img class="phero-bg" src="/assets/img/photos/apartment.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/apartment-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/apartment.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="768" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><div class="phero-shade"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/claim-appraisals/">Appraisals</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Co-op, Condo &amp; Commercial Appraisal</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Commercial Property Insurance Appraisal for Co-ops, Condos and Buildings</h1><p class="lede">Commercial property insurance appraisal for buildings with many stakeholders needs more than a good estimate; it needs a clean record that boards, managing agents and unit owners can rely on. We work with co-ops, condos and income properties across New York.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-orange" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost-l" href="/reach-us/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>Clients</dt><dd>Boards, managing agents, owners</dd></div><div><dt>Policies</dt><dd>Master and commercial property forms</dd></div><div><dt>Key issue</dt><dd>Who owns which repair</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Buildings with more than one policy</h2>
<p><strong>Commercial property insurance appraisal</strong> in a New York co-op or condo usually sits on top of a layered insurance picture. The master policy covers the building, unit owners carry HO-6 or co-op shareholder policies, commercial tenants have their own coverage, and the proprietary lease or condo declarations decide who repairs what. Before an appraisal demand is drafted, we map those layers so the panel values the right scope under the right policy.</p>
<p>Our guide to the <a href="/policyholder-guides/co-op-condo-master-policy-vs-ho6/">master policy versus HO-6</a> explains the split, and the <a href="/appraisal-calculators/co-op-condo-claim-splitter/">co-op and condo claim splitter</a> helps sort line items by responsible party.</p>
<h2>Properties we work on</h2>
<ul>
<li>Pre-war and post-war co-op buildings in Manhattan, the Bronx and Queens</li>
<li>Condo associations and garden-apartment complexes on Long Island and in Westchester</li>
<li>Mixed-use buildings with storefronts below and apartments above</li>
<li>Retail strips, small office buildings and warehouses upstate</li>
<li>Multifamily rental buildings, from Brooklyn walk-ups to Rochester and Buffalo apartment blocks</li>
</ul>
<h2>Issues that drive commercial disputes</h2>
<p>Commercial claims often turn on business income or rent loss periods, coinsurance clauses, code upgrades for older buildings, façade and roof systems, and whether a water loss that started in one unit spread through common elements. We document each category separately so the award is clear about what was valued and why.</p>
<p>Older New York buildings make these questions harder. A pre-war co-op may have plaster on terra-cotta block, steam heat risers that run through every apartment and a façade subject to Local Law 11 inspections in New York City. A Rochester or Buffalo apartment block may have decades of layered renovations behind its walls. Pricing a repair in those buildings means accounting for occupied-unit access, union or prevailing labor in some settings, protection of common hallways and elevators, and coordination with the managing agent’s building rules. We build those realities into the estimate rather than treating a building claim like a large house.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Board approvals</b>Many co-op and condo bylaws require a board vote before the building demands appraisal or signs an award. Build that timing into the plan, and have the board’s counsel review coverage questions the panel cannot decide.</div>
<h2>Communication with boards and managing agents</h2>
<p>We report in plain language the board can share with shareholders or unit owners: what was inspected, where the estimates differ, what the panel is likely to accept and what is outside appraisal entirely. Managing agents get a single point of contact for access scheduling, which matters in occupied buildings.</p>
<h2>Where this fits with other services</h2>
<p>Many building claims begin as water losses; see our <a href="/claim-appraisals/burst-pipe-water-damage-appraisal/">water damage appraisal</a> page for how unit-to-unit leaks are handled. For buildings in the five boroughs, our <a href="/where-we-appraise/manhattan/">Manhattan</a> page covers local considerations, and we also serve buildings in the other boroughs, on Long Island, in Westchester and upstate.</p>

<h2>Questions about co-op, condo &amp; commercial appraisal</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can a condo association and a unit owner both appraise the same loss?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes, if each has a policy with an appraisal clause and a dispute about amount. The two appraisals are separate, so clear scope boundaries help avoid paying twice or missing items.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal cover business income or lost rent?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Many commercial policies allow the amount of business income or rental loss to be appraised. Whether a loss period is covered at all is usually a coverage question.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who signs the demand for a co-op building?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Usually the corporation, acting through the board or an authorized officer. Check the bylaws and the policy’s named insured.</p></div></details></div>
